    'nuances' => array(
        'Never carry an English slash-plural or slash-alternative into Turkish. \'issue/s\' is simply \'sorunlar\', \'part/s\' is \'parçalar\', \'day/s\' is \'günler\'. Turkish orthography has no \'/lar\' convention, and reproducing it is one of the loudest machine-translation fingerprints — it appears most often in form help text and priority definitions.',
        'Field labels and buttons that START an action take an INDEFINITE, unmarked object: \'Destek talebi gönderin\', \'Servis talebi oluşturun\', \'Teklif isteyin\' — never the definite accusative (\'Destek talebini gönderin\'), which points at a specific request the visitor has not created yet. Use the definite accusative or a possessive only when the object already exists in context (\'Destek talebinizi gönderin\' on a confirmation screen).',
        'Do not repeat the same noun stem twice inside one short question or label. \'What type of request is this?\' becomes \'Talebiniz hangi kategoriye giriyor?\', not \'Bu talep ne tür bir taleptir?\'. When two adjacent form fields render to the same Turkish noun, differentiate them by recasting one as a category question (\'hangi kategoriye giriyor\') and the other as a description request (\'daha ayrıntılı olarak açıklayabilir misiniz\') so the visitor never sees two fields asking the same thing.',
        'Business-English abstractions must take the operational Turkish word, not the finance/IT homonym: asset (a machine on a service form) = \'ekipman\' or \'cihaz\', never \'varlık\'; embedded (inline in a production line) = \'entegre\' or \'hat içi\', never \'gömülü\' (that is embedded software); proximity (of service) = \'yerinde hizmet\', never bare \'yakınlık\'; history (of a company) = \'tarihçe\', never \'tarih\' (which reads as a calendar date); contact name (on a form) = \'Ad Soyad\' or \'Yetkili kişi\', never \'İletişim adı\'.',
        'Every option inside one <select>, and every label inside one labelled group (priority levels, request types, tabs), must share ONE capitalisation pattern — Turkish sentence case: \'Onarım ve saha/uzaktan servis\', \'Yedek parça ve tüketim malzemeleri\', \'Kalibrasyon / sertifikasyon\', \'Öncelik 1: Yüksek öncelik\'. Never mirror the English Title Case on some items and not others; after drafting a list, re-read it top to bottom and force one pattern.',
        'A product or capability concept keeps ONE Turkish noun everywhere, including in headings: analytics = \'analitik\' (or \'analitik çözümler\' when a heading needs a head noun), never \'analizler\', which means individual analyses. The same applies to resources hub = \'kaynak merkezi\' in nav, body and footer alike, and to service vs support: \'Service\' = \'servis\' and \'Support\' = \'destek\' — never swap one for the other inside a dropdown the customer is using to classify their own case.',
        'Established English trade loanwords that a Turkish engineer says out loud stay as they are; do not unpack them into descriptive Turkish: \'blower\' stays \'blower\' (never \'hava üfleyici\'), \'aktüatör\' is the noun (never the calque \'aktüasyon\'), and the same holds for HVAC, OBD, NVH, ADAS, dinamometre. Unpack an English compound only when the literal Turkish would otherwise be meaningless.',
        'Compound nouns written as one word in Turkish commercial usage must not be split: \'pazaryeri\' (marketplace), \'işbirliği\' (collaboration), \'kaynakça\'. Check current Turkish e-commerce/industry usage rather than mirroring the English two-word form.',
        'A standalone connector or label must be unambiguous out of context. On a rebrand or before/after banner, \'is now\' between two logos is \'yeni adıyla\', not bare \'artık\', which in isolation reads as negative polarity (\'no longer\'). Whenever a string will render alone between two graphics, choose the form that cannot be read as a negation.',
        'A registered division, product or entity name stays in English in EVERY surface once it is kept English in any one of them: \'Ascential Medical & Life Sciences bölümü\' in the nav, not \'Ascential tıbbi ve yaşam bilimleri bölümü\'. Translate only the generic head noun around it (\'bölümü\', \'ekibi\', \'platformu\').',
        'Never leave a bare adjective as a standalone link, tab or list label. \'Medical\' as an affiliate link is \'Medikal\' or \'Tıp\', never the attributive \'Tıbbi\'; \'Industrial\' is \'Sanayi\', never \'Endüstriyel\' on its own. A label must be a noun that can stand alone.',
        'A clause following a numeric stat must have an explicit subject and a finite predicate: \'projects and counting\' = \'13.000 proje ve sayısı artıyor\', not \'proje ve artmaya devam ediyor\', which leaves the verb without a subject. Rewrite English elliptical tags (\'and counting\', \'and growing\', \'and more\') as a short full Turkish clause.',
        'When an English sentence contains a relative clause (\'processes that support the production needs of X\'), keep it as a Turkish pre-nominal participle attached to its real head (\'... üretim ihtiyaçlarını destekleyen ... süreçlerini içerir\'). Do not split it off with a semicolon into a second coordinate clause — that leaves the new verb\'s subject ambiguous between the two preceding nouns.',
        'Press-release verbs are factual claims: \'Announces X as <title>\' = \'... olarak atandığını duyurdu\' or \'... olarak duyurdu\'; \'Promotes\' = \'yükseltti\'; \'Appoints\' = \'atadı\'; \'Names\' = \'atadı\'. Never upgrade an announcement to a promotion — \'yükseltti\' asserts an internal promotion the English does not claim, and it erases the distinction from the headlines that really do say \'Promotes\'.',
        'Use one apostrophe character — the straight ASCII \' — for every suffix on a proper noun, in every surface (menu card, news grid, body, footer). The same name must never appear as both Kime\'yi and Kime’yi, and the same English headline must produce exactly one Turkish rendering wherever it is reused, so never vary between \'... Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı olarak atadı\' and \'... Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcılığına atadı\'.',
        'Correlative \'ister ... ister ...\' must carry the particle on BOTH branches and the shared verb must follow the LAST branch. Write \'İster sıfırdan başlayalım ister mevcut teknolojilerden yola çıkalım, ...\' — never \'Sıfırdan başlayalım ister mevcut teknolojilerle başlayalım\'. English \'Starting with either X or Y\' and \'Whether it is X or Y\' translate left-to-right into exactly this broken shape, so after drafting any \'either/or\' or \'whether\' sentence, re-read it and check the first \'ister\' is present.',
        'Do not render English \'Our X\' as \'Bizim X-ımız\'. The possessive suffix already carries \'our\': write \'Amacımız\', \'Sonuçlarımız\', \'Değerlerimiz\'. Reserve the explicit \'Bizim\' for genuine contrast with someone else\'s. The same applies to \'Biz\' before a first-person verb.',
        'Job titles must preserve seniority exactly. \'Senior Vice President\' = \'Kıdemli Başkan Yardımcısı\'; plain \'Vice President\' = \'Başkan Yardımcısı\' — the two must never collapse into one form. \'Chief Technology Officer\' = \'Teknoloji Direktörü (CTO)\', never \'Teknoloji Müdürü\', which is middle management. \'Executive Vice President\' = \'İcra Başkan Yardımcısı\', not \'İcra Kurulu Başkan Yardımcısı\', which names a board office. When promoting someone, the dative goes on the POST, not the person: \'Başkan Yardımcılığına yükseltti\', never \'Başkan Yardımcısına yükseltti\'.',
        '\'Americas\' is the continent pair, not one country: render it \'Amerika kıtası\' or \'Amerika kıtasında\', never bare \'Amerika\', which a Turkish reader reads as the USA.',
        'Never reproduce an English hyphen chain word for word. \'<X>-as-a-service\' inverts in Turkish: \'hizmet olarak balanslama (BaaS)\', never \'balanslama-hizmet-olarak\'. The same applies to \'end-of-line\' = \'hat sonu\' (no genitive on \'hat\'), \'end-to-end\' = \'uçtan uca\', \'real-time\' = \'gerçek zamanlı\'. If the Turkish result contains stacked hyphens that a specialist could not read aloud, rewrite it.',
        '\'Inquiry\' in a sales or support context is \'talep\' (or \'başvuru\'), never \'sorgulama\', which means interrogation or a database query. Likewise a support \'case\' is a \'talep\', not a \'vaka\' (medical/legal). These labels sit on conversion buttons, so a dictionary-first choice here costs leads.',
        '\'Featuring X\' in a product label is \'X teknolojili\' or \'X teknolojisiyle donatılmış\', never the colloquial \'-li\' straight on the brand (\'Bauer\'li\'). Keep the register of a spec line at the level of the surrounding copy.',
        '\'Affiliates\' (group companies) is \'Bağlı kuruluşlar\' or \'İştirakler\'. Do not use \'Ortaklar\', which means business partners and collides with the separate term \'İş ortakları\' already used for \'Partners\'. Keep distinct English concepts on distinct Turkish words.',
        'Never invert who acts on whom. \'understand how our site is used\' is \'sitemizin nasıl kullanıldığını anlamak\', not \'sitenizi nasıl kullandığımızı anlamak\'. After translating any sentence with two participants, back-translate it mentally and confirm the same party is doing the same thing as in the English.',
        '\'Rebranded as X\' is \'adını X olarak değiştirdi\' or \'X adıyla yeniden markalandı\' — never \'yeniden markalaştı\', which is not a Turkish verb pattern for this. When a corporate-action verb has no obvious Turkish verb, use an \'adını ... olarak değiştirdi\' style paraphrase rather than inventing a suffixed loan.',
        'When an English heading is split across two styled spans (\'<span>Our</span> <span>purpose</span>\'), translate the phrase as a whole and put the single Turkish word in the span that carries the accent colour, leaving the other span present but empty. Never translate each span in isolation — that is what produced \'Bizim amacımız\'.',
        'Before finishing a sentence, find the main verb and check every object it governs carries the case that verb licenses. \'etkilemek\', \'artırmak\', \'azaltmak\', \'iyileştirmek\' all take the accusative: \'verimi artırır\', \'süresini, sağlığını, onarımları ve daha pek çok alanı etkileyebilir\'. A coordinated list of objects needs the accusative on EVERY item, not just the last, and no item may drift into the locative (\'alanda\') — that leaves a transitive verb with no object and is an immediate machine-translation tell.',
        'Never put the plural -lAr on a noun that follows a number or sits in a numeric stat counter, even when the English is plural: \'13.000 proje\', \'ana ülke\', \'küresel konum\', \'uzman çalışan\' — never \'ana ülkeler\' or \'küresel konumlar\'. For headcount use \'çalışan\' or \'kişi\', never \'insanlar\', which means human beings in the biological sense.',
        'English comma-appositive titles must be rebuilt with the scope first, and the comma removed: \'Service Manager, Americas\' = \'Amerika kıtası Servis Müdürü\'; \'Vice President of Technology, Aerospace & Industrials\' = \'Havacılık ve Sanayi Teknoloji Başkan Yardımcısı\'. Never leave a comma inside a Turkish job title — it is a pure English structure.',
        'Turkish noun compounds need the possessive marker on the head noun (izafet). \'landing gear test\' = \'iniş takımı testi\', \'metals automation\' = \'metal otomasyonu\', \'powertrain assembly, balance and test\' = \'güç aktarma organları montajı, balanslaması ve testi\'. A menu label or bullet ending in a bare \'test\', \'otomasyon\' or \'montaj\' with no suffix is ungrammatical. Conversely, do not leave a possessive suffix stranded on a coordinated list item that is not the head: \'yedek parça ve analitik\', not \'yedek parça ve analitiği\'.',
        'One English term gets exactly ONE Turkish rendering across the whole site, including when it appears in a nav label, a mega-menu item, a body sentence and the footer. Reviewers caught: \'metals automation\' as both \'metal işleme otomasyonu\' and \'metal otomasyon\'; \'Terms\' as both \'Koşullar\' and \'Şartlar\'; \'Leadership\' as both \'Liderlik\' and \'Yönetim\'; \'propulsion\' as both \'tahrik\' and \'itki\'; \'marketplace\' as both \'pazar\' and \'pazar yeri\'; \'end of line\' as both \'hattın sonu\' and \'üretim sonu\'; \'See how we bring it all together\' in three different wordings. Before delivering, group the strings that share an English term and force them to agree.',
        'A brand or product name is never half-translated, even inside a longer phrase: \'XL Machine\' stays \'XL Machine\' (never \'XL Makine\'), \'ADAS Garage\' stays \'ADAS Garage\' (never \'ADAS garajı\'), and the same goes for Cimat, Galileo, Kleinknecht, Lismar, Titan, Burke Porter, WinterPark, Bauer, Sentia, Ascential Care and Ascentialytics. If one item in a product list is a brand, leave it untouched and translate only the descriptive words around it.',
        'Use the shop-floor term, not the dictionary neighbour: field service = \'saha\' (never \'alan\', which is an area or a data field); machine down = \'arızalı\' / \'arızalandı\' (never \'kapalı\', which means switched off); rigid rotor = \'rijit rotor\' (never \'katı\'); roll texturing = \'tekstürleme\' (never \'doku işleme\', which reads as image processing); \'available service level agreements\' = \'mevcut hizmet düzeyi anlaşmaları\' (never \'kullanılabilir\', the software sense); build a machine or system = \'üretmek\' / \'geliştirmek\' (never \'inşa etmek\', which is for buildings).',
        'Translate English words that already have a Turkish equivalent used elsewhere on the same site. If \'Resources hub\' is \'Kaynaklar merkezi\', then \'Visit hub\' is \'Merkezi ziyaret edin\' and bare \'Hub\' is \'Merkez\' — never leave \'Hub\'ı\' with a Turkish suffix bolted onto an English stem when the Turkish word is already in use two lines away.',
        'Preserve the subject and the agent of the English sentence. \'Standing on the shoulders of our many brands, we design...\' keeps WE as the one standing: \'Burke Porter gibi birçok markamızın gücünü arkamıza alarak ... tasarlıyor ve üretiyoruz\'. Never insert a comitative \'-le/-la\' (\'şirketlerle\') that turns the company\'s own brands into third-party partners, and never re-attach a participle to the nearest noun instead of the sentence\'s real subject. Likewise \'when people are empowered\' keeps \'insanlar\' as the empowered party — \'İnsanlara ... güç verildiğinde dünyanın ilerlediğine inanıyoruz\' — never letting \'dünya\' occupy both roles.',
        'Attach the accusative to a proper noun ending in a vowel with the buffer consonant -y-: \'Kime\'yi\', \'Moretti\'yi\', \'Michalet\'i\' is wrong only where the final letter is a consonant — check the actual final LETTER, harmonise to the pronunciation, and use one apostrophe style (straight \') everywhere. The same name must never appear with two different suffixes or two different apostrophes on one page.',
        'Keep one date format across the whole site. Use DD.MM.YYYY (22.09.2026) in every surface — nav cards, news grid, press labels alike — and never mix it with the long form \'22 Eylül 2026\'. The thousands separator is a period: \'13.000\', never \'13,000\', which a Turkish reader parses as thirteen point zero.',
        'Keep the formal plural (siz) on every imperative, including short card buttons: \'Makaleyi okuyun\', \'Duyuruyu okuyun\', \'Tüm basın bültenlerini görün\'. A single \'-∅\' imperative (\'oku\', \'gör\') next to a \'-in\' imperative is the register flip reviewers cite as proof the page was never read end to end.',
        'An adverb of manner that modifies HOW we act must attach to the verb, not be dropped and not be turned into an attribute. \'allowing us to adapt swiftly\' = \'hızla uyum sağlamamıza olanak tanır\'; \'we engineer ... uncompromising possibilities\' = \'ödün vermeden ... olanaklar geliştiririz\'. Never render \'engineer\' as a verb by the periphrasis \'mühendislik yöntemiyle tasarlamak\'; use \'geliştirmek\', \'üretmek\' or \'tasarlamak\' directly.',
        'Turkish word order is verb-final also in short UI labels and search prompts: \'Search Ascential\' = \'Ascential\'de ara\', never \'Ara Ascential\'. Whenever a label starts with an English verb followed by an object, flip it before you translate.',
        'Do not flatten a differentiated claim word into a generic one: \'end-to-end\' = \'uçtan uca\' (not \'kapsamlı\'), \'mine data\' = \'verileri derinlemesine incelemek\' (not just \'analiz etmek\'), \'utilities construction\' = \'enerji ve altyapı tesisleri inşaatı\' (not the broader \'altyapı inşaatı\'), \'it all\' = \'her şeyi\' (not \'bunu\'). Each of these is the word that narrows or widens what the company is claiming.',
        'Capitalise the first character of every translated sentence, heading and CTA; a headline that opens lowercase is an instant proofreading failure regardless of the target language.',
        'Translate enumerations item for item: never insert a list element the source does not contain, and never repeat a term the governing head noun already carries.',
        'When the source coordinates two alternatives and one branch is itself a compound term, pick a connector that visibly separates the branches instead of stacking two conjunctions in one phrase.',
        'Build "ister… ister…" so every branch has the same shape and the shared verb sits at the END: "İster sıfırdan ister mevcut teknolojilerle başlayalım". Never put a finite verb in the first branch and leave the second as a bare noun or postpositional phrase.',
        'Express physical features and parts with -li or "…olan", never "…-e sahip": "kendi muylusu olan rotorlar", "dört kanallı sistem". Reserve "sahip olmak" for genuine ownership by a person or company.',
        'Before using a verb+noun idiom, check which case it licenses. "Güç kazanmak" takes an accusative nominalization ("başarma gücünü kazanmak"), not a dative infinitive ("başarmaya güç kazanmak"). If unsure, rewrite with a plain common verb.',
        'Use one Turkish term per concept across the whole file: bileşen (not komponent) for component, muylu for journal, merdane for roll, balanslama for balancing. Never alternate synonyms between strings.',
        'When a list of sector modifiers ends in a head noun ("…dâhil birçok sanayi sektörü"), keep every list item a bare modifier — "otomotiv, elektrik, pompa, savunma, kâğıt" — and do not repeat "sanayi" inside the list.',
        'Put the subject of a nominalized -DIK/-(y)EcEK clause in the genitive ("başkalarının vazgeçtiği", "ekiplerin doğrulamasına", "Dünyanın ilerlediğine"); leave it nominative only in temporal -DIğInDA adverbials ("insanlar … kazandığında").',
        'Preserve the English modal strength and register: keep hedges as hedges ("can significantly impact" → "önemli ölçüde etkileyebilir"), keep job titles and common nouns lowercase ("program sorumlusu"), and keep straight ASCII quotes and apostrophes before suffixes (Ascential\'ın).',
        'In subordinate clauses built with -DIK/-(y)EcEK + possessive (gördüğü, olduğu, yaptığı), always put that clause\'s own subject in the genitive: "başkalarının sınır gördüğü yerde", never "başkaları sınır gördüğü yerde". Apply it to every such clause in a sentence, not just the first.',
        'Never pass an English term through word-for-word when the Turkish result is meaningless — degree fields, job functions, product categories. Render the established Turkish name of the field and drop redundant English words ("science business analytics" → "iş analitiği").',
        'Keep every branch of "ister… ister…" in the same grammatical shape: all converbs, all infinitives, or all noun phrases sharing one verb (olun/olsun) at the end. Never pair a verb-bearing branch with a bare noun or postpositional phrase.',
        'Express physical features and components with -li or "…olan", not "sahip": "kendi muylusu olan rotorlar", "dört kanallı sistem" — not "…-e sahip". Reserve "sahip olmak" for genuine ownership by a person or company.',
        'Use only straight ASCII double quotes for quoted text in Turkish. Never emit German low quotes („ "), and never open with one mark and close with another.',
        'Do not reproduce English noun stacks with chained Turkish genitives or "…yelpazesinde". Convert "a wide range of X surfaces" to an adjective plus plural noun: "çok çeşitli merdane yüzeylerinde".',
        'Verify that a verb+noun collocation exists in Turkish with the complement you give it before using it; do not attach a dative infinitive to a fixed idiom ("başarmaya güç kazanmak"). If unsure, rewrite with a plain, common verb.',
        'Correlatives (ister...ister, hem...hem, ne...ne, gerek...gerek) are only correct when every branch ends in its own finite verb, or the single shared verb follows the LAST branch. A branch that ends in a bare noun or postpositional phrase ("...ister mevcut teknolojilerle,") is ungrammatical. Before delivering, read each correlative aloud branch by branch and complete the short one.',
        'Turkish is sentence case everywhere, headlines included. Capitalise only the first word of the sentence and genuine proper nouns. Postpositions (olarak, ile, için, gibi), verbs, job titles, technology names and common nouns stay lower case even in a press-release headline. Never mirror the capitalisation pattern of the English source.',
        'Punctuation marks must match at both ends. Use straight double quotes " " for Turkish quotation; never open with the German low-9 mark „ or mix mark families within one pair. Apply the same rule to parentheses, dashes and apostrophes.',
        'Never use \'sahip olmak\' for credentials, qualifications, opportunities or other abstractions. Degrees and diplomas are \'almak\'; opportunities and results are \'elde etmek\'; capabilities are \'kazanmak\' or \'kavuşmak\'. Reserve \'sahip olmak\' for concrete possession and for fixed technical attributes (kendi muylularına sahip rotorlar).',
        'An adjective that describes how WE work (uncompromising, relentless, dedicated, tireless) must become an adverbial on the verb, not an attribute of an inanimate noun. Write \'ödün vermeden ... üretiyoruz\', never \'ödün vermeyen olanaklar\'. Ask whether the noun could literally perform the behaviour; if not, move the modifier to the verb.',
        'When the English source is garbled, redundant or internally contradictory (\'science business analytics\', \'blank slate or existing technologies\'), translate the real referent rather than the words. Never emit a Turkish noun chain that a specialist could not parse. If a source word adds no meaning, drop it.',
        'Respect Turkish verb case-government and prefer one adjective to a stacked noun chain. Use the case the verb actually licenses (güce kavuşmak, gücü bulmak), and render \'a wide range of X\' as \'çok çeşitli X\', never as \'geniş bir X yelpazesi\', which produces three chained genitive nouns.',
        'In \'help someone do X\' constructions, the possessive on the verbal noun must agree with the person doing X, not with us: \'çözmenize / doğrulamanıza / azaltmanıza yardımcı oluyoruz\'. Writing \'-memize\' reverses the meaning and is a frequent, invisible error.',
        'Never strand an English adjective before a question word or another noun. Rebuild the modifier as a full attributive phrase attached to its head: \'Aerospace or Industrial challenges\' = \'havacılık veya sanayi alanındaki zorluklar\', not \'havacılık veya endüstriyel ... zorluklar\'.',
        'Always pick the shop-floor term over the generic dictionary word: roll (rolling/grinding line) = merdane, not rulo; journal = muylu; shaft = mil; tight tolerance = dar tolerans; rework = yeniden işleme. When a word has both an everyday and a machining sense, use the one a maintenance engineer would say out loud.',
        'Turkish is sentence case everywhere — headlines, bullets, buttons, job titles and technology names. Write \'girdap akımı\', \'yüzey dalgası\', \'tahribatsız muayene\', \'program sorumlusu\' in lower case. Capitalise only sentence-initial words and genuine proper nouns.',
        'Correlatives (\'ister...ister\', \'hem...hem\', \'ne...ne\', \'gerek...gerek\') must be structurally parallel: every branch carries its own verb, or every branch is a bare noun phrase. After drafting, re-read each correlative and complete the shorter branch.',
        'Swap English collocations for the Turkish pairing, not the Turkish dictionary word: sharp minds = parlak zihinler; deep expertise = engin/köklü uzmanlık; a wide range of = çok çeşitli; support offering = destek hizmetlerimiz (\'sunum\' = presentation); actionable = eyleme dönüştürülebilir. Never use \'sahip olmak\' for abstractions (fırsat, derece, imkân) — use elde etmek, almak, bulmak.',
        'Keep sibling list items in one grammatical shape: either all noun phrases or all finite sentences, never mixed within the same bullet group. Prefer the plain present or a participle in marketing bullets and reserve \'-mAktAdIr\' for specifications and legal text.',
        'Recreate slogans, taglines and CTAs for effect rather than word by word, and keep coordinated nouns consistently possessed: \'Impossible? Done.\' = \'İmkânsız mı? Başardık.\'; \'a promise and our standard\' = \'hem bir sözdür hem de standardımızdır\'. Avoid flat fillers (\'Tamam\') and avoid repeating a stem inside one phrase (endüstri ... endüstriyel → \'demiryolu dâhil birçok sanayi sektörü\').',
        'Never end a Turkish sentence or bullet with a bare adjective or loanword the way English does (\'— optimal\', \'— dedicated\'). Every line needs a finite Turkish predicate: \'... için idealdir\', \'... için özel olarak tasarlanmıştır\'.',
        'In -DIK / -EcEK subordinate clauses the subject must carry the genitive: \'başkalarının gördüğü yerde\', \'ekiplerin doğruladığı veri\' — never \'başkaları gördüğü yerde\'.',
        'Translate technical terms that already have an established Turkish equivalent: eddy current = girdap akımı, journal = muylu, NDT = tahribatsız muayene (gloss the acronym on first use, then reuse). Never leave one item of a list in English while translating its siblings.',
        'Do not use \'sahip olmak/sahiptir\' to render English \'have/hold\'. Use elde etmek, bulmak, almak, mezunu olmak. Reserve -DIr and -mAktAdIr for legal or specification text; marketing copy takes the plain present (-ir / -iyor).',
        'Correlatives must be complete and parallel: \'ister X ister Y (ister Z) olsun\' — each branch needs its own verb, and never mix \'ister\' with \'veya\' or \'ya da\'.',
        'Turkish headlines, titles and buttons use sentence case: capitalise only the first word and proper nouns. Never mirror English Title Case.',
        'Attach suffixes to foreign proper nouns with an apostrophe and harmonise to the Turkish pronunciation, not the spelling: College\'den (not College\'dan), Ascential\'in, Sentia\'ya, Technologies\'e.',
